        "content": "{\n  \"status\": \"unmet\",\n  \"material_usefulness\": \"supporting\",\n  \"useful_material\": \"该研究提供了 945 名接受 durvalumab 治疗患者的回顾性数据，报告了抗生素使用的类别分布（β-内酰胺类 51.3%，氟喹诺酮类 39.3%）及其与生存期的关联。主要发现是早期抗生素暴露（起始前后 30 天内）与总生存期（OS）缩短相关（7.2 vs 9.8 个月），而晚期暴露（>30 天）未显示负面影响甚至与 OS 改善相关。此外，研究包含一项小鼠实验，使用左氧氟沙星（氟喹诺酮类）在晚期给药时增强了抗 PD-L1 疗效。\",\n  \"auxiliary_material\": \"研究定义为单臂、1/2 期研究（Study 1108）的回顾性分析，涵盖 18 种肿瘤类型（主要是 NSCLC 和尿路上皮癌）。抗生素数据包括途径、持续时间和类别，但在提供的摘要文本中，人类队列的生存分析主要按暴露时间（早期/晚期/无）分层，未明确展示按抗生素类别分层的具体生存对比结果。小鼠实验模型为 CT26 肿瘤植入 Balb/c 小鼠。\",\n  \"remaining_gap\": \"提供的文本未明确报告不同抗生素类别（如β-内酰胺类 vs 氟喹诺酮类）对人类 ICI 疗效影响的直接对比数据。虽然记录了类别分布，但生存分析结果主要基于时间窗（早期 vs 晚期），无法据此判断哪一类抗生素相对更安全或负面影响更小。成功标准中要求的'至少 2 项比较不同抗生素类别的研究'在此材料中未得到满足，且材料本身未明确声明现有证据仅支持'广谱抗生素'整体效应（尽管结果暗示时间因素可能更关键）。\",\n  \"outline_action\": \"在综述中利用此材料说明抗生素暴露的时间依赖性可能比类别差异更显著，或作为背景证据支持早期抗生素普遍有害的共识。需继续检索明确按抗生素类别分层分析 ICI 疗效的独立研究，或查找系统评价是否已汇总过类别特异性数据。若无法找到，需在综述中说明当前证据多集中于'广谱抗生素'整体效应或时间窗效应，类别特异性结论尚不明确。\"\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "The user wants me to analyze a captured paper text in relation to a specific literature-planning gap about antibiotic class specificity in ICI (Immune Checkpoint Inhibitor) therapy.\n\n**Gap Question:** \"不同类别抗生素（如β-内酰胺类 vs 氟喹诺酮类）对 ICI 疗效的负面影响是否存在显著差异，是否有特定类别相对安全？\" (Are there significant differences in the negative impact on ICI efficacy between different antibiotic classes (e.g., beta-lactams vs. fluoroquinolones)? Is there a specific class that is relatively safe?)\n\n**Success Criteria:**\n1. Find at least 2 studies comparing the impact of different antibiotic classes on ICI efficacy.\n2. OR explicitly state that existing material only supports the overall effect of 'broad-spectrum antibiotics'.\n\n**Material Provided:**\n- A text containing metadata abstracts and PDF text snippets from what appears to be a conference proceeding or journal supplement (J Immunother Cancer 2020;8(Suppl 3)).\n- Specifically, Abstract 674 titled \"LATE ANTIBIOTIC ADMINISTRATION DURING DURVALUMAB TREATMENT MAY BE ASSOCIATED WITH CLINICAL BENEFIT\" by Siddharth Sheth et al. (AstraZeneca/UNC-CH).\n- There is also Abstract 673 about microbiome mapping, and Abstract 675 about NK cells, but the relevant one for the gap is Abstract 674.\n- The review_planning_B content provides orientation on how this paper was previously categorized (time-window focus, mechanism hints).\n\n**Task:**\n1. Describe what the supplied paper studied and found.\n2. Explain how that material may help with the requested gap.\n3. Judge the gap status (fulfilled, partial, unmet, unjudgeable).\n4. Judge material usefulness (direct, supporting, none).\n5. Identify useful_material and auxiliary_material.\n6. Describe remaining_gap and outline_action.\n7. Output as a single JSON object.\n\n**Analysis of the Paper (Abstract 674):**\n- **Study Design:** Retrospective analysis of a single-arm Phase 1/2 study (Study 1108) with 945 patients receiving durvalumab. Also includes a translational in vivo mouse analysis.\n- **Exposure:** Early antibiotics (30 days prior to 30 days after initiation) vs. Late antibiotics (>30 days after initiation) vs. No antibiotics.\n- **Antibiotic Classes Reported:** Results state \"ß-lactams (51.3%) and fluoroquinolones (39.3%) were most commonly prescribed overall.\"\n- **Findings:**\n    - Early antibiotic exposure associated with reduced median Overall Survival (mOS) compared to no exposure (7.2 vs 9.8 months).\n    - Late antibiotic exposure associated with *improved* mOS vs no exposure (19.8 vs 9.8 months).\n    - Mouse model: Late levofloxacin (a fluoroquinolone) with anti-PD-L1 improved survival compared to control.\n- **Relevance to Gap:**\n    - The gap asks about *class specificity* (Beta-lactams vs. Fluoroquinolones) regarding *negative impact*.\n    - This study reports the *overall* distribution of classes (51.3% beta-lactams, 39.3% fluoroquinolones).\n    - It reports the impact of *timing* (Early vs. Late).\n    - **Crucial Limitation:** The abstract text provided *does not* explicitly state a statistical comparison of efficacy outcomes *stratified by antibiotic class*. It says \"Demographics, infection type, and antibiotic data (route, duration, class) were collected.\" and \"Median PFS and OS were compared between no antibiotics, early antibiotics, and late antibiotics\". It does *not* explicitly say \"Beta-lactams had X effect vs Fluoroquinolones had Y effect\". It mentions the mouse study used Levofloxacin (fluoroquinolone).\n    - The review_planning_B notes: \"小鼠实验中使用的左氧氟沙星剂量和给药方式与人体的临床用法可能存在差异，且单一抗生素的结果不能代表所有抗生素类别。\" (Mouse study used only levofloxacin... single antibiotic result cannot represent all classes).\n    - The text says \"ß-lactams (51.3%) and fluoroquinolones (39.3%) were most commonly prescribed overall.\" It does not provide a subgroup analysis of OS/PFS by class within the human cohort in the provided text.\n    - Therefore, this study does *not* fulfill the success criterion of \"Find at least 2 studies comparing the impact of different antibiotic classes\". It provides data on classes used, but not necessarily a comparative outcome analysis between classes in the human data (based on the provided text). It supports the \"broad-spectrum\" or \"timing\" narrative more than the \"class specificity\" narrative, although it acknowledges class data was collected.\n    - Wait, I need to check if there is any comparison implied. \"Early antibiotic exposure was associated with reduced mOS...\". It doesn't break this down by class in the abstract text provided.\n    - So, for the specific gap about *class specificity differences*, this paper is **supporting** (it shows classes were collected and early use is bad generally, mouse used fluoroquinolone) but does not **directly** answer if one class is safer than another in humans.\n    - Actually, the success criteria say: \"Find at least 2 studies comparing... OR explicitly state that existing material only supports 'broad-spectrum antibiotics' overall effect.\"\n    - Since this study (and the provided text) does not show a class comparison result, it contributes to the evidence that we might only have support for the overall effect (or timing effect), unless another paper in the set does. But I must judge based on *this* material's contribution to the gap.\n    - The gap status should reflect whether the *gap itself* is resolved by this material. Since the gap asks for class comparison and this material primarily discusses timing (Early vs Late) with class distribution mentioned but not compared for outcomes, the gap remains **unmet** or **partial** regarding class specificity.\n    - However, the instruction says: \"Do not call the whole gap fulfilled while a stated success criterion remains open.\"\n    - Success Criterion 1: Find at least 2 studies comparing different antibiotic classes. (This study does not clearly compare classes in outcomes, only timing).\n    - Success Criterion 2: Or explicitly state existing material only supports 'broad-spectrum'. (This study supports the timing nuance, but regarding class, it lists them without outcome comparison).\n    - So the gap status regarding *this material's ability to fill the gap* is **unmet** (it doesn't provide the class comparison). But the instruction asks for `status` of the gap *based on the supplied paper text*? No, it says \"Read the supplied captured paper text, gap question, success criteria... Explain any direct contribution... Keep the primary gap judgment separate from material usefulness\".\n    - Usually, in this workflow, `status` refers to whether the *gap* is resolved by the *available material provided in this turn*. Since the material does not provide the class comparison, the gap is not fulfilled by this material.\n    - `material_usefulness`: This material is **supporting**. It confirms early antibiotics are harmful (consistent with broad-spectrum view) and introduces timing. It mentions class distribution but lacks class-specific outcome comparison. It is useful for the review to discuss *why* class specificity is hard to determine or to note that timing might be more critical than class (as per the study's conclusion).\n    - `useful_material`: The findings on Early vs Late timing, the distribution of classes (beta-lactams, fluoroquinolones), and the mouse model using levofloxacin.\n    - `auxiliary_material`: The metadata about the study design (Phase 1/2, durvalumab, 945 patients), the definition of early/late.\n    - `remaining_gap`: We still lack direct comparative data between beta-lactams and fluoroquinolones regarding ICI efficacy in humans from this text. We don't know if one class is safer.\n    - `outline_action`: Need to search for studies that specifically stratify outcomes by antibiotic class, or acknowledge that current evidence (including this study) focuses more on timing than class.\n\n**Refining the Judgment:**\n- **Status:** `unmet` (The specific question about class specificity differences is not answered by this text; it answers a timing question).\n- **Material Usefulness:** `supporting` (It provides context on antibiotic usage patterns and the general negative effect of early use, which is relevant background, but doesn't solve the class specificity puzzle).\n- **Constraint Check:** \"Do not call the whole gap fulfilled while a stated success criterion remains open.\" -> Since I cannot confirm 2 studies comparing classes from this text, status cannot be fulfilled.\n- **Constraint Check:** \"Keep the primary gap judgment separate from material usefulness\".\n- **Constraint Check:** \"Refer to the paper as 'this study' or 'the authors'\".\n- **Constraint Check:** \"Do not infer study design... from silence\". The text says \"retrospectively analyzed\".\n- **Constraint Check:** \"Multiple data sources... do not by themselves establish transfer\". The mouse study is separate.\n\n**Drafting the Content:**\n- **",
